{
 "lead": "Delta modulation (DM) is the simplest differential coder: one bit per sample. At each clock tick it compares the input with a staircase built from past bits and sends 1 if the input is higher, 0 if lower; the staircase then moves one step Δ up or down. The hardware is a comparator, a clock and an integrator. Adaptive delta modulation (ADM) changes Δ with the signal slope, which makes DM usable for speech in low-cost voice links.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#differential-pcm-and-adaptive-dpcm\">DPCM</a>, of which DM is the 1-bit case, and the slope of a sinusoid.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "How delta modulation works",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The transmitter keeps a staircase approximation x̃ of the input. At sample n it compares x[n] with the previous staircase value x̃[n − 1] and sends b[n] = 1 if x[n] ≥ x̃[n − 1], else 0. The staircase then moves: x̃[n] = x̃[n − 1] + Δ for a 1 and − Δ for a 0. This is DPCM with the prediction x̂[n] = x̃[n − 1] and a two-level quantizer ±Δ."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "R<sub>b</sub> = f<sub>s</sub>",
     "where": "One bit per sample, so the bit rate equals the sampling rate. To track speech, f<sub>s</sub> is many times the Nyquist rate (32 to 64 kHz for telephone speech)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The receiver rebuilds the same staircase from the bits with an accumulator (an integrator in analog form), then a lowpass filter at f<sub>m</sub> smooths it. It needs only the bit clock: there are no word boundaries. Because each bit only says “higher” or “lower”, the staircase never settles on a steady input: when the gap is smaller than Δ, a step overshoots and the next one comes back."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Slope overload and granular noise",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The staircase can rise at most Δ per sample, a slope of Δf<sub>s</sub> volts per second. When the input rises faster, the staircase falls behind and sends a long run of equal bits while the gap grows: <b>slope overload</b>. For x(t) = A sin(2πft) the steepest slope is 2πfA, at the zero crossings, so there is no overload when"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Δ f<sub>s</sub> ≥ 2π f A",
     "where": "Δ in volts, f<sub>s</sub> in samples/s, f the tone frequency in Hz, A the peak amplitude in volts."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "On a slow or flat input the staircase hunts up and down by Δ around it: <b>granular noise</b>, the DM form of quantization noise. A large Δ cures overload but raises granular noise; a small Δ does the reverse. The figure and the second worked example show both on one tone: the staircase hunts at the peak and lags on the falling slope."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Adaptive DM and the comparison with PCM",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>ADM</b> changes the step from the bit pattern. A run of equal bits means the staircase is falling behind, so the step is multiplied by a factor K &gt; 1 (often 1.5 or 2); alternating bits mean it is hunting, so the step is divided by K, within limits Δ<sub>min</sub> and Δ<sub>max</sub>. The receiver sees the same bits and repeats the same rule, so no extra bits are sent. CVSD, continuously variable slope DM, is the version used for voice."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a tone at the largest amplitude that avoids overload, DM gives SNR = 3f<sub>s</sub>³/(8π²f<sup>2</sup>W), where W is the receiver filter bandwidth. With f = 1 kHz, W = 4 kHz and f<sub>s</sub> = 64 kHz this is 2490, or 34.0 dB, while 8-bit PCM at the same 64 kbit/s gives 49.9 dB. Doubling f<sub>s</sub> adds only 9 dB to DM. So DM wins on simplicity and robustness to single bit errors (one error moves the output by only 2Δ), and PCM wins on quality per bit."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A 1 kHz sinusoid of peak 2 V is delta modulated with Δ = 0.1 V and f<sub>s</sub> = 32 kHz. (a) Find the largest slope of the input and of the staircase. (b) Does slope overload occur? (c) Find the smallest f<sub>s</sub>, and the smallest Δ at 32 kHz, that avoid it.",
   "steps": [
    "Input: 2πfA = 2π × 1000 × 2 = 12 566 V/s.",
    "Staircase: Δf<sub>s</sub> = 0.1 × 32 000 = 3200 V/s.",
    "12 566 &gt; 3200 V/s, so slope overload occurs near every zero crossing.",
    "Smallest f<sub>s</sub> = 2πfA/Δ = 12 566/0.1 = 125 664 samples/s, about 125.7 kHz.",
    "Or keep 32 kHz and raise the step: Δ ≥ 12 566/32 000 = 0.393 V, at the cost of more granular noise."
